Problem Statement
Given two sorted arrays arr1[] and arr2[] and an integer k, return the kth smallest element from the combined sorted array. In an interview, you can explain it like this: Since both arrays are sorted, we can merge them similar to Merge Sort. While merging, keep counting elements. The moment we reach the kth element, return it. Time Complexity: O(N + M) Space Complexity: O(N + M) int[] merged = new int[n + m];
// Merge both arrays
return merged[k - 1];
Notice something interesting: For Median of Two Sorted Arrays, we partitioned arrays such that: Left Half | Right Half
For Kth Element: Exactly k elements must lie on the left side.
The partition idea remains exactly the same. Whenever you see: Two Sorted Arrays Kth Smallest Element Logarithmic Solution Expected Think…

Problem Statement
Given two sorted arrays nums1 and nums2, return the median of the two sorted arrays. The overall run time complexity should be: O(log(min(N,M)))
In an interview, you can explain it like this: Since both arrays are sorted, we can merge them into a single sorted array and then directly compute the median from the merged array. Time Complexity: O(N + M) Space Complexity: O(N + M) int[] merged = new int[n + m];
// Merge both arrays
return median;
Do we really need the merged array? No. We only care about: Left Half Right Half
such that: All elements in Left Half <= All elements in Right Half
This is where partition-based Binary Search comes in. Whenever you see: Two Sorted Arrays Median O(log N) expected Think: Binary Search on Partition For total elements: n + m
